      </article>

      <!-- 9: Gardiner et al. 2004 -->
      <article class="reference-result-card">
        <div class="reference-result-meta">
          <span>2004</span><span>Research Article</span><span>Plant Physiology</span><span>DOI</span><span>PubMed</span>
        </div>
        <h3><a href="https://doi.org/10.1104/pp.103.034538" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anchoring 9,371 maize expressed sequence tagged unigenes to the bacterial artificial chromosome contig map by two-dimensional overgo hybridization</a></h3>
        <p class="reference-result-authors">Gardiner J, Schroeder S, Polacco ML, Sanchez-Villeda H, Fang Z, Morgante M, Landewe T, Browne K, Davis G, Coe EH, et al.</p>
        <p class="reference-result-citation">Plant Physiol. &bull; 134\(4\) &bull; 1317-1326 &bull; doi:10.1104/pp.103.034538</p>
        <p class="reference-result-abstract">Two-dimensional overgo hybridization strategy used to anchor 9,371 EST unigenes directly to BAC physical contigs, linking transcribed gene space to chromosome locations prior to whole-genome sequencing.</p>
        <div class="reference-result-links">
          <a href="https://doi.org/10.1104/pp.103.034538" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DOI &#8599;</a>
          <button class="reference-copy-id" type="button" data-copy-value="10.1104/pp.103.034538">Copy DOI</button>
          <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15064373/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PubMed &#8599;</a>
          <button class="reference-copy-id" type="button" data-copy-value="15064373">Copy PMID</button>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 9: FAQS -->
  <section id="assembly-faqs" aria-labelledby="assembly-faqs-heading">
    <div class="mgdb-section-heading">
      <div><h2 id="assembly-faqs-heading">Frequently Asked Questions \(FAQs\)</h2></div>
      <p>Common questions regarding maize reference genome definitions, assembly coordinate mapping, and annotations.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="mgdb-panel">
    <div class="assembly-faq-list">
      <details class="assembly-faq-item" open>
        <summary class="assembly-faq-question">What is a Representative Reference Genome?</summary>
        <div class="assembly-faq-answer">
          <p>A reference genome is a haploid representation of a species' DNA sequence with a defined coordinate system, version identifier, and structural annotations. B73 is the internationally recognized representative reference genome for <i>Zea mays</i>, serving as the common coordinate system for genetic maps, variant catalogs, and pan-genomic comparisons.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="assembly-faq-item">
        <summary class="assembly-faq-question">What are the main changes between RefGen_v4 and Zm-B73-REFERENCE-NAM-5.0?</summary>
        <div class="assembly-faq-answer">
          <p>Zm-B73-REFERENCE-NAM-5.0 is an advanced de novo assembly generated using PacBio Sequel long-read technology validated by high-resolution BioNano optical maps. Contig N50 jumped from 1.18 Mb in v4 to 25.6 Mb in v5, resolving previously unplaced scaffolds, complex repetitive element arrays, and centromeric regions.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="assembly-faq-item">
        <summary class="assembly-faq-question">Why was a preliminary v5 annotation released, and which set should I use?</summary>
        <div class="assembly-faq-answer">
          <p>A preliminary annotation \(<code>Zm00001e.1</code>\) was released alongside the initial v5 assembly to provide early data access. It was subsequently superseded by the official annotation, <code>Zm00001eb.1</code>, which features refined gene boundaries and sequentially numbered gene models across chromosomes. <strong>Always use <code>Zm00001eb.1</code> for current research and publications.</strong></p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="assembly-faq-item">
        <summary class="assembly-faq-question">How can I translate coordinates between assembly versions?</summary>
        <div class="assembly-faq-answer">
          <p>Chain files between v4 and v5 are available in the <a class="mgdb-link" href="https://download.maizegdb.org/Zm-B73-REFERENCE-NAM-5.0/chain_files/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MaizeGDB v5 Chain Files Directory</a> and can be used with standard tools like <strong>LiftOver</strong> or <strong>CrossMap</strong>. For v2 to v3 translation, use the <a class="mgdb-link" href="http://ensembl.gramene.org/tools.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ensembl Assembly Converter</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="assembly-faq-item">
        <summary class="assembly-faq-question">How do I identify the Filtered Gene Set \(FGS\) in RefGen_v3?</summary>
        <div class="assembly-faq-answer">
          <p>In the <code>5b+</code> gene build, former Filtered Gene Set models are explicitly classified as protein-coding models in the GFF3 and FASTA files.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="assembly-faq-item">
        <summary class="assembly-faq-question">Where can I find legacy resources from MaizeSequence.org?</summary>
        <div class="assembly-faq-answer">
          <p>Historical files, gene builds, and FTP dumps from MaizeSequence.org are permanently preserved in the <a class="mgdb-link" href="ftp://ftp.gramene.org/pub/gramene/maizesequence.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gramene MaizeSequence FTP Archive &#8599;</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 10: CORPUS STATISTICS \(SECOND TO LAST ITEM\) -->
  <section id="assembly-overview" aria-labelledby="assembly-overview-heading">
